  <section class="about">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">About Insights</p>
      <h1>How economists form judgment</h1>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="about-lead">Insights publishes long conversations with economists: where a research life began, how the work is actually done, and what a scholar is willing to judge in public.</p>
      <h2>Jingbang Lunce</h2>
      <p>The series is produced by the Chinese Economists Society. The Chinese title, 《经邦论策》, names an old ambition: to think about the polity through the craft of economics. Season One recorded eight conversations in Chinese. Season Two will continue that work, and will also invite English-speaking scholars.</p>
      <h2>Method</h2>
      <p>The interviews are long, lightly edited, and kept in full. We ask how a question was found, not only what the paper concluded. The language of the conversation is the language of the record. For a Chinese interview, this English site carries an editorial brief and a link back to the Chinese text. For an English interview, the English page is the record; a Chinese editorial account will follow.</p>
      <h2>Season Two</h2>
      <p>Season Two is organized around three questions: origin, craft, and judgment. We are inviting scholars whose work has changed how a field sees a problem — whether or not that problem is China. If you are reading this because you received a note from us, this archive is the context in which the conversation would appear.</p>
      <small>This site is an internal preview.</small>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
